  "query_preview": "..\n\nCumulus and towering cumulus are beginning to form along the\nterrain. Suitable moisture is in place with satellite derived \nPWATs around 1.0 to 1.75 inches, higher values Sells westward. \nHigh pressure overhead is centered over New Mexico and western \nTX today leading to southeast flow in the mid levels helping to \npull moisture in from the Gulf of California. As surface heating\ncontinues today and low level drying occurs, an inverted V \nsounding will develop leading to DCAPE values around 1500 to \n1700 J/kg. There will be a fair bit of mid-level shear as well, \n25 to 35 kts, with decent updraft helicity thus there is the \npotential for strong, longer- lived storms to develop. Storms \ntoday will be focused to the Arizona/ Mexico international \nborder and the Arizona/ New Mexico border. Stronger storms more \nlikely be located along the Arizona/ Mexico international \nborder. CAMs continue to have storms beginning to develop \nbetween 12 and 2 pm, initially on the higher terrain then moving\nwest to northwest. \n\nTomorrow, most of the ingredients seen today will be present.\nMid level shear will remain elevated at 25 to 35 kts, DCAPE of \n1500-1800 J/kg, and PWATs between 1.0 to 1.6 inches. Storms \nagain will be focused to the Arizona/ Mexico international \nborder and the the Arizona/ New Mexico border. Wednesday, \nchances for storms and showers begin to diminish as the high \npressure center begins to move westward. By Thursday, high \ntemperatures will be 7 to 12 degrees above normal leading to \nMajor to Extreme HeatRisk. Therefore, an Extreme Heat Warning \nhas been issued for most of southern Arizona (with the exception\nof the higher terrain) from Thursday morning through Sunday \nevening. At this level of HeatRisk, everyone should take proper \nprecautions to prepare for the heat as anyone is at risk for \nheat related illnesses if not staying hydrated or cool. Friday \nand Saturday look to be the hottest days in the forecast during\nthis stretch, with a 55% to 65% chance of 110 degrees for \nlocales Tucson westward both days. Chances for storms and \nshowers returns early next week as the center of high pressure \noscillates eastward.",
